// Private Core source part: HTTP/2 (RFC 9113) for core.http.client, with
// no I/O of its own. An H2Exchange is one request on a connection of its
// own. h2_start queues the client's preface, its SETTINGS, the request's
// HEADERS and as much of its body as the flow-control windows allow in
// `out`. h2_feed takes the bytes the server sent: it acks the server's
// SETTINGS and PINGs, sends the rest of the body as WINDOW_UPDATEs open the
// windows, decodes the header blocks (HPACK, RFC 7541) and gathers the
// response. The caller moves `out` and the server's bytes over a TCP stream
// (h2c with prior knowledge) or a TLS stream that negotiated `h2`.
use core

/// The client connection preface (RFC 9113 section 3.4).
H2_PREFACE: "PRI * HTTP/2.0\r\n\r\nSM\r\n\r\n"

// Frame types (RFC 9113 section 6).
H2_DATA: 0

H2_HEADERS: 1

H2_RST_STREAM: 3

H2_SETTINGS: 4

H2_PUSH_PROMISE: 5

H2_PING: 6

H2_GOAWAY: 7

H2_WINDOW_UPDATE: 8

H2_CONTINUATION: 9

// Frame flags.
H2_END_STREAM: 1

H2_ACK: 1

H2_END_HEADERS: 4

H2_PADDED: 8

H2_PRIORITY: 32

// Settings (RFC 9113 section 6.5.2).
H2_SETTING_ENABLE_PUSH: 2

H2_SETTING_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE: 4

H2_SETTING_MAX_FRAME_SIZE: 5

/// The flow-control window every stream and the connection start with.
H2_INITIAL_WINDOW: 65535

/// The largest frame payload either side takes before SETTINGS raise it.
H2_INITIAL_FRAME: 16384

/// The largest flow-control window (2^31 - 1).
H2_MAX_WINDOW: 2147483647

/// The largest frame payload a server may announce (2^24 - 1).
H2_MAX_FRAME: 16777215

/// The dynamic table size the decoder allows (SETTINGS_HEADER_TABLE_SIZE's
/// default, which the client never changes).
H2_TABLE_SIZE: 4096

/// The most bytes a response body may have.
H2_BODY_MAX: 1048576

/// The most bytes one response's header blocks may have.
H2_HEADER_BLOCK_MAX: 65536

/// The one stream an exchange opens.
H2_STREAM: 1

/// The bit lengths of the HPACK Huffman code of each symbol, 0 to 255 and
/// EOS (RFC 7541 Appendix B). The code is canonical: the codes of one
/// length are consecutive, in symbol order, so the lengths alone give it.
HPACK_HUFFMAN_BITS: [Int]{
    13,
    23,
    28,
    28,
    28,
    28,
